            "explanation": "divided = split (phân chia công việc nhà).",
            "passage": "<b>WHO DOES THE CHORES? SHARING FAIRLY</b>\nIn most homes, chores are shared, but rarely equally. Statistics Canada reports that men now spend about 1.9 hours a day on housework, which is 24 minutes more than thirty years ago, yet women still spend about 2.8 hours, almost an hour more. In Britain, a study by University College London of around 8,500 couples found that only seven per cent <u><b>divided</b></u> the housework equally. Even when both partners have full-time jobs, women are far more likely to do many hours of household work each week.\nThe gap lies not only in the amount of work but also in its type. Statistics Canada notes that women tend to do routine and repetitive tasks such as cooking, cleaning, laundry and shopping, whereas men more often do occasional tasks such as taking out the rubbish, repairing the house and mowing the lawn. Routine tasks must be done every day, so they leave less free time. The London researchers add that many couples simply follow the patterns they saw in their parents’ homes, a habit that leaves women with a “second shift” when they come home from work.\nFortunately, attitudes are changing. <b>[I]</b> Most men in the London study agreed that housework should be shared, even though they did less than five hours a week themselves. <b>[II]</b> The gap was smallest in the few homes where the woman was the main breadwinner; there, men did as much as women. <b>[III]</b> Families can begin by talking openly about who does what and by giving every member, including children, a regular task. <b>[IV]</b> Small changes like these can turn chores from a source of argument into a habit of cooperation.\nSharing housework matters for more than fairness. Children who watch their parents divide chores fairly are likely to see this pattern as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> may repeat it in their own homes one day. Chores also teach responsibility, cooperation and teamwork, so a home in which everyone contributes is not only cleaner but also happier.\n<i>(Adapted from HuffPost Canada – report on Statistics Canada’s “Women in Canada” data, and Earth.com – “Study shows that women are still doing most of the housework”)</i>"
