            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON FAMILY LIFE</b>\nIn recent years, sharing household chores and strengthening family bonds has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of family members recognise the importance of household chores, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>divided</b></u> these practices into their daily routines at home. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to divide housework fairly.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that teach children responsibility and teamwork, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how household chores can reduce stress on working mothers and strengthen family bonds.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should divide housework fairly, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role at home. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting sharing household chores and strengthening family bonds matters for the long-term future. Young people who see family members model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, household chores not only teach children responsibility and teamwork today but also reduce stress on working mothers and strengthen family bonds for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 1: Family Life)</i>"
