            "passage": "<b>MAKING FAMILY LIFE PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to divide housework fairly may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed household chores help family members teach children responsibility and teamwork. At the same time, these practices reduce stress on working mothers and strengthen family bonds and foster stronger cooperation at home.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in household chores has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>regular</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Family members can start with simple steps at home, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind sharing household chores and strengthening family bonds become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>habit</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 1: Family Life)</i>"
