            "passage": "<b>MAKING HUMANS AND THE ENVIRONMENT PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to adopt an eco-friendly lifestyle may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed green habits help citizens and students cut down on single-use plastic waste and save energy. At the same time, these practices reduce our household carbon footprint and protect local ecosystems and foster stronger cooperation in our community.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in green habits has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>sustainable</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Citizens and students can start with simple steps in our community, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind adopting a green lifestyle and reducing our carbon footprint become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>lifestyle</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 2: Humans and the Environment)</i>"
