            "passage": "<b>MAKING MUSIC PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to appreciate both folk music and modern pop may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed musical performances help young musicians and audiences connect people from different cultures and relieve daily stress. At the same time, these practices inspire teenagers to learn traditional musical instruments and foster stronger cooperation on the concert stage.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in musical performances has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>popular</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Young musicians and audiences can start with simple steps on the concert stage, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind enjoying traditional and modern music shows become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>melody</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 3: Music)</i>"
