            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON GENDER EQUALITY</b>\nIn recent years, promoting gender equality in education and careers has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of men and women recognise the importance of career opportunities, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>treated</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in the workplace. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to promote equal rights and fair pay.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that allow both men and women to pursue their dream careers, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how career opportunities can reduce poverty and boost economic growth for the whole society.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should promote equal rights and fair pay, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in the workplace. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting promoting gender equality in education and careers matters for the long-term future. Young people who see men and women model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, career opportunities not only allow both men and women to pursue their dream careers today but also reduce poverty and boost economic growth for the whole society for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 6: Gender Equality)</i>"
