            "passage": "<b>MAKING VIET NAM AND INTERNATIONAL ORGANISATIONS PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to cooperate closely with international organisations may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed international programmes help member countries and youth delegates improve healthcare and education for disadvantaged children. At the same time, these practices promote trade, peacekeeping and cultural exchange worldwide and foster stronger cooperation across Southeast Asia.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in international programmes has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>active</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Member countries and youth delegates can start with simple steps across Southeast Asia, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind Viet Nam's active participation in international organisations become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>aim</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 7: Viet Nam and International Organisations)</i>"
