            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON PROTECTING THE ENVIRONMENT</b>\nIn recent years, protecting biodiversity and stopping deforestation and wildlife trade has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of rangers and students recognise the importance of environmental programmes, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>protected</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in national parks. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to protect natural habitats and biodiversity.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that prevent deforestation and protect endangered animals from poaching, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how environmental programmes can maintain the natural balance of ecosystems and reduce air pollution.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should protect natural habitats and biodiversity, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in national parks. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting protecting biodiversity and stopping deforestation and wildlife trade matters for the long-term future. Young people who see rangers and students model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, environmental programmes not only prevent deforestation and protect endangered animals from poaching today but also maintain the natural balance of ecosystems and reduce air pollution for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 9: Protecting the Environment)</i>"
