            "explanation": "Trong ngữ cảnh đoạn 1, 'respected' đồng nghĩa với 'valued'.",
            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON THE GENERATION GAP</b>\nIn recent years, understanding generational differences and bridging the generation gap has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of parents and teenagers recognise the importance of generational differences, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>respected</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in extended and nuclear families. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to respect different generational viewpoints.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that reduce family conflicts over screen time, hairstyles and career choices, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how generational differences can build mutual respect between grandparents, parents and Gen Z children.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should respect different generational viewpoints, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in extended and nuclear families. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting understanding generational differences and bridging the generation gap matters for the long-term future. Young people who see parents and teenagers model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, generational differences not only reduce family conflicts over screen time, hairstyles and career choices today but also build mutual respect between grandparents, parents and Gen Z children for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 2: The Generation Gap)</i>"
