            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON CITIES OF THE FUTURE</b>\nIn recent years, smart cities, green skyscrapers and sustainable urban living has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of city dwellers and planners recognise the importance of smart technologies, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>connected</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in future urban centres. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to build sustainable and liveable smart cities.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that use AI sensors to save energy and eliminate traffic jams, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how smart technologies can expand rooftop gardens and pedestrian zones for a greener environment.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should build sustainable and liveable smart cities, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in future urban centres. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting smart cities, green skyscrapers and sustainable urban living matters for the long-term future. Young people who see city dwellers and planners model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, smart technologies not only use AI sensors to save energy and eliminate traffic jams today but also expand rooftop gardens and pedestrian zones for a greener environment for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 3: Cities of the Future)</i>"
