            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON ASEAN AND VIET NAM</b>\nIn recent years, ASEAN youth cooperation, cultural exchange and leadership has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of young delegates from ASEAN nations recognise the importance of cultural exchange programmes, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>selected</b></u> these practices into their daily routines across the ASEAN region. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to participate actively in ASEAN youth projects.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that promote solidarity, mutual understanding and peace among member states, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how cultural exchange programmes can develop leadership, communication and teamwork skills for youth.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should participate actively in ASEAN youth projects, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role across the ASEAN region. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting ASEAN youth cooperation, cultural exchange and leadership matters for the long-term future. Young people who see young delegates from ASEAN nations model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, cultural exchange programmes not only promote solidarity, mutual understanding and peace among member states today but also develop leadership, communication and teamwork skills for youth for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 4: ASEAN and Viet Nam)</i>"
