            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON PRESERVING OUR HERITAGE</b>\nIn recent years, preserving cultural and natural heritage sites across Viet Nam has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of young people and local artisans recognise the importance of heritage sites, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>preserved</b></u> these practices into their daily routines across Viet Nam. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to preserve and promote our national heritage.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that pass down traditional folk arts like Quan Họ and Đờn Ca Tài Tử, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how heritage sites can protect ancient monuments and scenic landscapes from damage.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should preserve and promote our national heritage, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role across Viet Nam. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting preserving cultural and natural heritage sites across Viet Nam matters for the long-term future. Young people who see young people and local artisans model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, heritage sites not only pass down traditional folk arts like Quan Họ and Đờn Ca Tài Tử today but also protect ancient monuments and scenic landscapes from damage for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 6: Preserving Our Heritage)</i>"
