            "explanation": "Trong ngữ cảnh đoạn 1, 'completed' đồng nghĩa với 'finished'.",
            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON EDUCATION OPTIONS FOR SCHOOL-LEAVERS</b>\nIn recent years, choosing between university study and vocational training after high school has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of school-leavers and parents recognise the importance of education options, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>completed</b></u> these practices into their daily routines at universities and vocational colleges. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to choose the right educational pathway after graduation.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that gain practical hands-on skills through apprenticeships, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how education options can earn formal degrees or trade qualifications that match career goals.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should choose the right educational pathway after graduation, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role at universities and vocational colleges. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting choosing between university study and vocational training after high school matters for the long-term future. Young people who see school-leavers and parents model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, education options not only gain practical hands-on skills through apprenticeships today but also earn formal degrees or trade qualifications that match career goals for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 7: Education Options for School-Leavers)</i>"
