            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON SOCIAL ISSUES</b>\nIn recent years, addressing peer pressure, cyberbullying and body shaming among teens has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of students, counsellors and parents recognise the importance of social issues, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>addressed</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in schools and online. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to stand up against bullying and support victims.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that protect teenagers from cyberbullying, body shaming and negative peer pressure, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how social issues can create a safe, empathetic and supportive school environment for all.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should stand up against bullying and support victims, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in schools and online. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting addressing peer pressure, cyberbullying and body shaming among teens matters for the long-term future. Young people who see students, counsellors and parents model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, social issues not only protect teenagers from cyberbullying, body shaming and negative peer pressure today but also create a safe, empathetic and supportive school environment for all for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 9: Social Issues)</i>"
