            "explanation": "Trong ngữ cảnh đoạn 1, 'admired' đồng nghĩa với 'respected'.",
            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON LIFE STORIES WE ADMIRE</b>\nIn recent years, admiring inspiring life stories, national heroes and innovators has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of young readers and historians recognise the importance of inspiring biographies, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>admired</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in history and modern life. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to admire and learn from great life stories.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that learn about the determination and selflessness of national heroes, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how inspiring biographies can inspire teenagers to overcome obstacles and contribute to society.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should admire and learn from great life stories, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in history and modern life. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting admiring inspiring life stories, national heroes and innovators matters for the long-term future. Young people who see young readers and historians model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, inspiring biographies not only learn about the determination and selflessness of national heroes today but also inspire teenagers to overcome obstacles and contribute to society for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 1: Life Stories We Admire)</i>"
