            "explanation": "Trong ngữ cảnh đoạn 1, 'blended' đồng nghĩa với 'combined'.",
            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON A MULTICULTURAL WORLD</b>\nIn recent years, celebrating cultural diversity, traditional festivals and global cuisines has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of students from diverse backgrounds recognise the importance of cultural traditions, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>blended</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in a multicultural world. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to respect and celebrate cultural diversity.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that help people overcome culture shock and appreciate cultural diversity, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how cultural traditions can preserve national cultural identity in an age of globalisation.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should respect and celebrate cultural diversity, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in a multicultural world. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting celebrating cultural diversity, traditional festivals and global cuisines matters for the long-term future. Young people who see students from diverse backgrounds model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, cultural traditions not only help people overcome culture shock and appreciate cultural diversity today but also preserve national cultural identity in an age of globalisation for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 2: A Multicultural World)</i>"
