            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON GREEN LIVING</b>\nIn recent years, reducing plastic waste, composting leftovers and adopting green living has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of students and households recognise the importance of green-living habits, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>recycled</b></u> these practices into their daily routines at school and at home. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to practise green living and waste recycling.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that reduce plastic packaging sent to landfills and save water and electricity, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how green-living habits can turn fruit peels and leftovers into nutrient-rich compost for gardens.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should practise green living and waste recycling, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role at school and at home. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting reducing plastic waste, composting leftovers and adopting green living matters for the long-term future. Young people who see students and households model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, green-living habits not only reduce plastic packaging sent to landfills and save water and electricity today but also turn fruit peels and leftovers into nutrient-rich compost for gardens for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 3: Green Living)</i>"
