            "passage": "<b>MAKING URBANISATION PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to plan urban expansion sustainably may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed urban developments help city planners and residents upgrade public transport, schools and hospitals for growing populations. At the same time, these practices reduce traffic congestion, housing shortages and air pollution and foster stronger cooperation in rapidly growing cities.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in urban developments has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>affordable</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. City planners and residents can start with simple steps in rapidly growing cities, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind urbanisation trends, infrastructure challenges and sustainable city growth become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>resident</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 4: Urbanisation)</i>"
