            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON THE WORLD OF WORK</b>\nIn recent years, job requirements, working conditions and career preparation has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of job applicants and employers recognise the importance of job opportunities, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>recruited</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in the modern workplace. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to prepare a strong CV and develop workplace skills.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that gain valuable on-the-job training and communication skills, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how job opportunities can earn a fair wage while learning time management and responsibility.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should prepare a strong CV and develop workplace skills, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in the modern workplace. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting job requirements, working conditions and career preparation matters for the long-term future. Young people who see job applicants and employers model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, job opportunities not only gain valuable on-the-job training and communication skills today but also earn a fair wage while learning time management and responsibility for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 5: The World of Work)</i>"
