            "passage": "<b>MAKING ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to use artificial intelligence responsibly and effectively may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed AI applications help students, teachers and engineers provide personalised learning feedback and automate routine tasks. At the same time, these practices help doctors analyse medical scans and assist elderly people at home and foster stronger cooperation in schools and smart homes.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in AI applications has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>capable</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Students, teachers and engineers can start with simple steps in schools and smart homes, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind applications of artificial intelligence in education, healthcare and homes become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>platform</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 6: Artificial Intelligence)</i>"
