            "passage": "<b>MAKING THE WORLD OF MASS MEDIA PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to use mass media critically and responsibly may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed mass media channels help journalists and netizens deliver breaking news quickly and connect communities worldwide. At the same time, these practices help citizens verify credible sources and stop the spread of fake news and foster stronger cooperation across digital and print media.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in mass media channels has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>credible</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Journalists and netizens can start with simple steps across digital and print media, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind digital media, fact-checking misinformation and responsible media use become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>source</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 7: The World of Mass Media)</i>"
