            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON WILDLIFE CONSERVATION</b>\nIn recent years, protecting endangered wildlife, rehabilitating primates and sea turtles has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of conservationists and rangers recognise the importance of conservation programmes, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>rescued</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in nature reserves and rescue centres. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to conserve endangered wildlife and their natural habitats.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that rescue poached primates and sea turtles and release them back into the wild, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how conservation programmes can protect forest habitats and enforce strict anti-poaching laws.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should conserve endangered wildlife and their natural habitats, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in nature reserves and rescue centres. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting protecting endangered wildlife, rehabilitating primates and sea turtles matters for the long-term future. Young people who see conservationists and rangers model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, conservation programmes not only rescue poached primates and sea turtles and release them back into the wild today but also protect forest habitats and enforce strict anti-poaching laws for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 8: Wildlife Conservation)</i>"
