            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON CAREER PATHS</b>\nIn recent years, exploring career paths, developing soft skills and adapting to automation has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of school-leavers and career advisers recognise the importance of career paths, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>automated</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in the changing job market. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to build both technical expertise and soft skills.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that develop soft skills like critical thinking, teamwork and adaptability, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how career paths can keep up with technological changes and choose a fulfilling career.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should build both technical expertise and soft skills, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in the changing job market. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting exploring career paths, developing soft skills and adapting to automation matters for the long-term future. Young people who see school-leavers and career advisers model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, career paths not only develop soft skills like critical thinking, teamwork and adaptability today but also keep up with technological changes and choose a fulfilling career for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 9: Career Paths)</i>"
