            "passage": "<b>MAKING CAREER PATHS PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to build both technical expertise and soft skills may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed career paths help school-leavers and career advisers develop soft skills like critical thinking, teamwork and adaptability. At the same time, these practices keep up with technological changes and choose a fulfilling career and foster stronger cooperation in the changing job market.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in career paths has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>in demand</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. School-leavers and career advisers can start with simple steps in the changing job market, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind exploring career paths, developing soft skills and adapting to automation become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>passion</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 9: Career Paths)</i>"
