            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON LIFELONG LEARNING</b>\nIn recent years, lifelong learning, adult night classes and keeping the mind sharp has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of adult learners and educators recognise the importance of lifelong learning courses, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>acquired</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in community centres and online. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to maintain the habit of lifelong learning.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that broaden horizons, boost memory and keep the brain sharp in old age, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how lifelong learning courses can help working adults update professional skills and adapt to new technology.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should maintain the habit of lifelong learning, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in community centres and online. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting lifelong learning, adult night classes and keeping the mind sharp matters for the long-term future. Young people who see adult learners and educators model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, lifelong learning courses not only broaden horizons, boost memory and keep the brain sharp in old age today but also help working adults update professional skills and adapt to new technology for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 10: Lifelong Learning)</i>"
