            "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 16–20) You will hear a man talking on the radio about local sporting events happening over the next week.\nMr Daniel: Good morning, sports fans! If you’re looking to get active or watch some top-flight action this week, our town has plenty lined up.\nStarting on Saturday morning at eight o’clock, we have the annual Charity Road Cycling Challenge. Now, entries closed last Friday so you can’t register on the day anymore, and you can ride either solo or in a group. What makes it suitable for different fitness levels is that riders can choose between two routes: a twenty-kilometre family loop or a gruelling eighty-kilometre hill circuit.\nAll weekend, the regional Athletics Championships will be taking place at our historic riverside stadium. Local television won’t be broadcasting it live, so the only way to catch the action is in person—and it’s well worth buying a ticket, because three Olympic medallists and several world-class sprinters and distance runners are competing as part of their warm-up for the world championships.\nOn Sunday morning, head over to the municipal aquatic centre for the Spring Diving Cup. This competition has been running for over thirty years, and while nobody’s expecting a world record to fall, all eyes will be on fourteen-year-old local schoolgirl Mia Reynolds, a hugely talented teenager from our own town who won the national junior title last month.\nFrom Monday through Friday, the County Tennis Tournament returns to West Park. It’s our most famous sporting event, and although ticket prices have been frozen at last year’s rates, every single seat for the finals is already sold out. However, stay tuned at the end of this broadcast, because we have two pairs of VIP tickets to give away to listeners who can answer our simple trivia question!\nFinally, if you want something more relaxing on Sunday afternoon, instructors are running free open-air yoga sessions on the lawn in Central Park. There’s no need to book in advance—just bring a mat—and the routines are designed for everyone from five-year-olds to grandparents in their eighties!",
